From e9f86ac2b6befdd4f771865ed6c33d6040e80e16 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AshFall UI <ui@ashfall.local>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:21:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Settings: add charging control to the battery page

AshFall #140 v1: the manual charging-separation toggle on
Settings > Battery > Charging control, as the second sequential patch on
the battery page (after #137). One row sits in the PlayTime category,
after the PlayTime row, and opens a sub-screen with the toggle, the
firmware support state and the 80 %/10 % battery-care line.

State lives in persist.ashfall.batt.charge_sep (1 | 0). The switch
applies it immediately, refuses to enable below 20 %, and Settings
re-applies it at boot from a BOOT_COMPLETED receiver added here (Settings
is system_app; hwd cannot reach a vendor HIDL service from system_ext).
An ACTION_POWER_DISCONNECTED receiver clears the state and restores
charging, and the page registers the same broadcast while it is open.

The control is the retail vendor factory HAL: a reflective call to
vendor.qti.hardware.factory@1.0::IFactory.getService() and
chargerEnable(boolean). A missing class or method, a null service and a
RemoteException all show "Not supported on this firmware" and disable the
toggle instead of leaving a dead switch. Every error path fails open:
the property is cleared first and charging is restored, so a crash, a
firmware change or a missing HAL can never leave the battery not
charging. The boot path does nothing when the stored state is off, so a
missing HAL cannot cause a boot loop.

Copy follows #155: one short sentence per summary, no property names,
sysfs paths or HAL jargon in user text, and the single real warning
(fuel-gauge drift on long separation sessions) kept as two lines.

No QS tile, default off, no dependency on ashfall-hwd.
